# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 has "all_contingent ∧ contingent_not_always → nothing_once"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "that which is possible not to be at some time is not" and "everything is possible not to be" are true, which the second allows; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 has "contingent_not_always → (all_contingent → ◇nothing_once)"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 with no modality; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "could" does not say which it means
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "that which is possible not to be at some time is not" and "everything is possible not to be" are true and "at one time nothing was in existence" and "possibly, at one time nothing was in existence" are false. Is S4, "Therefore, if everything is possible not to be, then at one time there could have been nothing in existence.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 have "begin_by_existing" and "nothing_once ∧ begin_by_existing → nothing_now"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 has "begin_by_existing"; they differ when only "at one time nothing was in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "at one time nothing was in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true and "even now nothing would be in existence" is false. Is S5, "Now if this were true, even now there would be nothing in existence, because that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 have "¬nothing_now"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 has "begin_by_existing → (nothing_once → nothing_now)" and "¬◇nothing_now"; they differ when only "at one time nothing was in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S6 is read aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 with no modality; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "at one time nothing was in existence" and "that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing" are true and "even now nothing would be in existence" and "possibly, even now nothing would be in existence" are false. Is S6, "Therefore, if at one time nothing was in existence, it would have been impossible for anything to have begun to exist; and thus even now nothing would be in existence--which is absurd.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S8 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 has "necessary_exists → all_caused ∨ self_necessary"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 have "necessary_exists → all_caused ∨ ¬all_caused"; they differ when only "there exists something the existence of which is necessary" is true, which the second allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S8: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "there exists something the existence of which is necessary" is true and "every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another" and "there exists some being having of itself its own necessity, causing in others their necessity" are false. Is S8, "But every necessary thing either has its necessity caused by another, or not.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 has "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "all_caused → infinite_regress"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 has "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "all_caused → infinite_regress" and "necessary_exists ∧ ¬all_caused → self_necessary"; they differ when only "everything is possible not to be", "there exists something the existence of which is necessary" and "there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another" are true, which the first allows; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 has "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "necessary_exists ∧ all_caused → infinite_regress" and "necessary_exists ∧ ¬all_caused → self_necessary"
aquinas-third-way.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 take for granted "if every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another, then there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 does not
aquinas-third-way.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another, then there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 P3 (S3): the sentence says "impossible", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "that which is possible not to be at some time is not"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 P10 (S11): the sentence has no word of "there exists some being having of itself its own necessity, causing in others their necessity": is it the sentence P10 came from?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 P3 (S3): the sentence says "impossible", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "that which is possible not to be at some time is not"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.2 P9 (S11): the sentence has no word of "there exists some being having of itself its own necessity, causing in others their necessity": is it the sentence P9 came from?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 P3 (S3): the sentence says "impossible", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "that which is possible not to be at some time is not"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/or: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 P8 (S8): the sentence says "either ... or", which often means one and not both, but P8 allows both: it reads "either every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another or it is not so that every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another"; if not both, say so with & !(... & ...)
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-third-way.logic.r2.3 P10 (S11): the sentence has no word of "there exists some being having of itself its own necessity, causing in others their necessity": is it the sentence P10 came from?
aquinas-third-way.logic.r2: argue/verdicts: valid as aquinas-third-way.logic.r2 reads it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict
